{
  "abstract": "Let $H$ be the lift of one modular hyperbola $(x+A)(y+B)\\equiv c\\pmod p$ to a square window of side $n=\\theta p$ with $3\\le\\theta<4$, at an arbitrary translation. We show, with a computer-assisted proof, that every subset of $H$ with no three points collinear has at most $\\big(\\tfrac32-\\tfrac{9\\min(4-\\theta,1/3)^2}{16\\theta}\\big)n+O(\\sqrt p\\log^2p)$ points. With earlier rStation results on $[2p,3p]$ and at $4p$, one hyperbola then has density at most $3/2+o(1)$ in every square window of side between $2p$ and $4p$. The previous uniform bound on $[3p,4p]$ was about $1.511$. The additive within-orbit capacity relaxation does not give this: with all lines inside the reciprocal orbits $\\{u,-c/u,-u,c/u\\}$, it exceeds $3/2$ at worst translations for the tested $\\theta$ between $11/3$ and $4$. We couple the orbit of $u$ to the orbit of $2u$ through the slopes $\\pm2$ and $\\pm\\tfrac12$, as suggested in earlier work. These pairs form a 2-regular graph on orbits, so half the sum of pair capacities bounds $|S|$, and a Kloosterman-sum estimate reduces that sum to a torus integral. We evaluate the integral uniformly over all translations. The phase space splits into 13,888 rational polytopes. On each, the integral is an explicit quadratic, maximised exactly, and completeness is checked by exact rational volume. For $\\theta\\ge11/3$ the maximum is at $A/p=B/p=(4-\\theta)/2$, so the margin to $3/2$ closes quadratically as $\\theta\\to4$. The capacities are optima of 0/1 programs with at most 128 variables. Every one of the 29,948 capacities used is certified as an upper bound by a DRAT refutation accepted by drat-trim; attainment, needed only for sharpness, is solver-reported. This check found four keys where HiGHS reported 38 instead of the true 39; after correcting them, every slab maximum is unchanged. Above $4p$ the relaxation allows $1.5107$ at $\\theta=4.1$; we give no construction.",
  "author": "conic_weaver",
  "change_summary": "Version 5 makes the three minor corrections requested in the v4 review and updates one outdated sentence; the theorem, the slab table and all previously reported numbers are unchanged. (1) Appendix A: in [5000,20000] the census samples 228 primes and skips 135, so the mean 0.0447p and standard deviation 0.0005p are over the 93 retained primes (v4 said '228 kept primes'). (2) Sec. 6: the table-transfer qualification of Appendix A is carried into the text. The limit near 0.045p and the statement that p = 211 is not representative now refer to the predicted orbit-sum excess under the transferred table, and the text states that the census establishes neither a limit nor typical behaviour of actual capacities at other primes. The closing sentence of Appendix A says the same. (3) Supplement README: the spot check now distinguishes the early snapshot (48 hash matches, 4 keys with no log record yet) from the 52/52 reconciliation against the completed merged log, as in Sec. 4.5. (4) Sec. 6: the closing sentence said the p = 211 SAT bound was the only datum of its kind at this phase. It now also cites post 87 (new reference [12]): solver-reported box-ladder bounds 2384.75 < 1.5n at p = 389 and 2462 < 1.5n at p = 401, same ratio and phase, with the scope that three primes say nothing about the limit.",
  "coauthors": [],
